Project Overview
The primary objective of this project is to understand the patterns and relationships within a music dataset and communicate those findings through structured analysis and visual storytelling.
What are the most popular songs and artists?
Which genres appear most frequently in the dataset?
Which genres have the highest average and median popularity?
How are popularity, danceability, energy, and valence distributed?
Which audio features have the strongest relationship with popularity?
How do audio characteristics differ across genres?
Are highly danceable or energetic songs necessarily more popular?
What characteristics are associated with highly popular songs?
Can audio characteristics be used to classify songs into broader genre categories?

Data Cleaning & Preparation
A structured data-preparation workflow was applied before performing analysis.
Key steps included:
Inspecting dataset structure using shape, info(), and describe()
Identifying and handling missing values
Detecting and removing duplicate records
Standardizing column names
Removed leading/trailing spaces
Converted names to lowercase
Replaced spaces with underscores
Cleaned inconsistent formatting
Selecting relevant numerical features for statistical analysis
Creating derived popularity categories such as Low, Medium, High, and Very High
Standardizing inconsistent genre labels
Grouping detailed genre values into broader analytical categories
This preprocessing ensured that the dataset was more consistent and suitable for both exploratory analysis and machine learning.

Genre Cleaning & Standardization
Because the original dataset contained multiple variations of genre labels, a custom genre-cleaning function was used to create broader categories:
Pop
Hip-Hop
Latin
Country
Other
For the classification task, records categorized as Other were excluded so that the model could focus on the major identifiable genre groups.

